Give Determinate Nix's thread-locals the default TLS model.

Determinate Nix marks seventeen thread-locals in libexpr and libutil
`[[gnu::tls_model("initial-exec")]]`, for a faster access on the
evaluator's hot path. Each library that uses one is then flagged
STATIC_TLS, and its whole TLS block must be placed in the static TLS
area that glibc sizes when the process starts.

`nix` links the libraries at start, so that works. huggorm reaches them
through a Python extension, which the interpreter dlopens, and glibc
keeps only a small reserve for a library opened later. libnixexpr's
block is 3800 bytes, mostly toml11's thread-local parser caches, and
the import fails:

    libnixexpr.so: cannot allocate memory in static TLS block

`GLIBC_TUNABLES=glibc.rtld.optional_static_tls` enlarges the reserve,
but glibc reads it once when the loader starts, so a library cannot set
it at import. With the default model every library loads under dlopen.

